Given the head of a linked list. A linked list contains a cycle if its last node is connected to a previous node. If the given list contains a cycle, return the length of the cycle. Otherwise, return 0.
Note: Internally, the driver code uses an integer x to represent the position (1-based indexing) of the node to which the last node is connected. 
If x = 0, it means last node points to null which indicating there is no loop.

Examples:

Input: 
Output: 4
Explanation: There exists a loop in the linked list 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5, the length of the loop is 4.

Input:
Output: 3
Explanation: There exists a loop in the linked list 19 -> 33 -> 10, the length of loop is 3.

Input:
Output: 0
Explanation: There is no loop.
Constraints:

1 ≤ no. of nodes in head ≤ 105
1 ≤ head.node->data ≤ 104
0 ≤ x < head.size()

Code:
/* Structure of Linked List Node
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int x) {
        data = x;
        next = null;
    }
}*/

class Solution {
    public int lengthOfLoop(Node head) {
        // code here
        if(head == null || head.next == null){
            return 0;
        }
        
        Node slow = head;
        Node fast = head;
        
        int count = 0;
        
        while(fast != null && fast.next != null){
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
            
            if(slow == fast){
                Node temp = slow;
                // count = 0;
                
                do{
                    temp = temp.next;
                    count++;
                }while(slow != temp);
                return count;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
